  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is countercurrent heat exchange?",
      "a": "It is when two flows run side by side in opposite directions, so heat passes from the warm one to the cold one all along the way. In a penguin's legs, warm blood going down heats cold blood coming up.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a heat exchanger work in animals?",
        "What is a countercurrent system?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why don't penguins' feet freeze?",
      "a": "Blood still reaches the feet, but it arrives cool, so the feet stay just above freezing. Penguins can also widen or narrow blood vessels to send a little more warm blood if the feet get too cold.",
      "asks": [
        "How cold are penguin feet?",
        "Do penguins have cold feet?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do penguins move their feet if they are so cold?",
      "a": "Most of the muscles that work the feet are up in the warm body and pull on long tendons, like strings. Tendons work fine when cold, and there is little muscle in the foot to keep warm.",
      "asks": [
        "Are there muscles in penguin feet?",
        "Why are penguin feet mostly tendons?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a penguin keep its body warm on the ice?",
      "a": "Warm blood leaving the body hands its heat to the cold blood returning from the feet, so the heat comes back to the body instead of being lost into the ice.",
      "asks": [
        "Why don't penguins lose heat through their feet?",
        "How does the heat get back to the body?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do other animals use the same trick?",
      "a": "Yes. Ducks, gulls, Arctic foxes, dolphin flippers and whale flukes all use countercurrent blood vessels to stop heat leaking out of thin body parts in the cold.",
      "asks": [
        "Why don't ducks' feet freeze?",
        "Which animals have countercurrent blood flow?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Penguins keep their feet at just above freezing using countercurrent heat exchange between arteries and veins in their legs.",
    "Ducks and gulls standing on ice use the same countercurrent blood flow in their legs."
